WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.418 --> 00:00:08.328
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.328 --> 00:00:10.348
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:13.298
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.308 --> 00:00:15.128
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.128 --> 00:00:17.748
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.758 --> 00:00:19.788
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.788 --> 00:00:21.748
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:25.388
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are going

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.808
to one of my favourite places, not just in

00:00:27.808 --> 00:00:30.737
life, but for this show: and that's Italy.

00:00:31.298 --> 00:00:33.368
And today, it's the story of one

00:00:33.368 --> 00:00:35.198
of Italy’s most controversial

00:00:35.258 --> 00:00:37.638
artists: Pier Paolo Pasolini.

00:00:38.218 --> 00:00:40.618
He was a poet, a novelist, an

00:00:40.628 --> 00:00:43.398
intellectual, a filmmaker, a Marxist,

00:00:43.568 --> 00:00:46.318
a man loved and loathed in equal

00:00:46.318 --> 00:00:49.628
measure, and his death more than 50

00:00:49.628 --> 00:00:52.778
years ago is still shrouded in mystery.

00:00:53.328 --> 00:00:55.568
This is also a request from a wonderful

00:00:55.568 --> 00:00:58.392
member from Italy, so Carlo, thank you

00:00:58.392 --> 00:01:00.068
very much for this great suggestion.

00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:01.280
I hope you'll enjoy it.

00:01:02.028 --> 00:01:04.158
OK, let's not waste a minute,

00:01:04.438 --> 00:01:06.368
and get right into it.

00:01:07.968 --> 00:01:11.338
At 1:30 in the morning of the 2nd of

00:01:11.338 --> 00:01:16.068
November, 1975, a police car is driving

00:01:16.078 --> 00:01:19.618
along the coastal road outside Ostia, a

00:01:19.648 --> 00:01:22.598
seaside town on the outskirts of Rome.

00:01:23.568 --> 00:01:26.998
In the dead of night, the officers see the

00:01:26.998 --> 00:01:29.858
headlights of a car speeding towards them,

00:01:30.278 --> 00:01:32.768
on the wrong side of the road, no less.

00:01:33.718 --> 00:01:35.448
They stop the car, a

00:01:35.468 --> 00:01:39.257
silver-grey Alfa Romeo GT 2000.

00:01:40.298 --> 00:01:42.588
Behind the wheel is a boy:

00:01:43.118 --> 00:01:45.808
Giuseppe Pelosi, Pino for short.

00:01:46.798 --> 00:01:48.788
He is seventeen years old,

00:01:49.138 --> 00:01:51.108
too young to legally drive.

00:01:51.938 --> 00:01:53.988
He's taken to the police station,

00:01:54.338 --> 00:01:57.118
where he says he stole the car.

00:01:58.178 --> 00:02:01.558
He seems anxious, concerned, and

00:02:01.838 --> 00:02:04.408
about one thing in particular: a

00:02:04.408 --> 00:02:06.982
ring he says he left inside it.

00:02:07.598 --> 00:02:09.758
A large ring, engraved with

00:02:09.758 --> 00:02:12.198
the words "United States Army."

00:02:13.618 --> 00:02:15.568
Police search the car,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.158
but no ring can be found.

00:02:19.188 --> 00:02:21.608
They do, however, find the

00:02:21.618 --> 00:02:23.588
vehicle registration documents.

00:02:23.998 --> 00:02:26.008
The car belongs to a man

00:02:26.258 --> 00:02:28.188
called Pier Paolo Pasolini.

00:02:29.758 --> 00:02:32.048
The police contact Pasolini's mother

00:02:32.268 --> 00:02:34.598
and sister, who he lives with, to let

00:02:34.598 --> 00:02:36.968
them know that his car has been stolen.

00:02:38.458 --> 00:02:41.448
There is even grimmer news to come.

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:45.008
At 6.30 that morning, the

00:02:45.008 --> 00:02:48.908
morning of November 2nd, 1975, a

00:02:48.908 --> 00:02:51.288
woman is walking home in Ostia.

00:02:52.188 --> 00:02:54.998
It is All Souls' Day, the Catholic

00:02:55.028 --> 00:02:57.488
feast for the dead, which, given

00:02:57.488 --> 00:02:59.688
what she will find, makes this all

00:02:59.698 --> 00:03:02.328
sound closer to fiction than reality.

00:03:03.638 --> 00:03:05.668
She sees something on the ground.

00:03:06.258 --> 00:03:09.018
At first it looks like a bundle of old

00:03:09.028 --> 00:03:12.788
clothes, but then she sees arms and legs.

00:03:13.488 --> 00:03:16.288
On closer inspection, it is a human

00:03:16.288 --> 00:03:19.448
body, the body of a middle-aged man.

00:03:20.178 --> 00:03:22.398
He is barely recognisable; he

00:03:22.398 --> 00:03:24.448
has been beaten so savagely

00:03:24.708 --> 00:03:26.748
that his face is unidentifiable.

00:03:27.648 --> 00:03:29.898
It looks like he's been run over

00:03:30.128 --> 00:03:32.368
by some kind of heavy vehicle.

00:03:33.608 --> 00:03:34.708
She calls the police.

00:03:35.368 --> 00:03:38.398
The dead man, they discover, is Pier

00:03:38.438 --> 00:03:41.308
Paolo Pasolini, one of the country's great

00:03:41.328 --> 00:03:44.428
intellectuals, poets, and filmmakers.

00:03:45.438 --> 00:03:48.378
At almost exactly the same time as

00:03:48.378 --> 00:03:50.858
she files the report, the news goes

00:03:50.868 --> 00:03:52.858
out on the national broadcaster:

00:03:53.538 --> 00:03:56.738
Pier Paolo Pasolini found dead.

00:03:58.058 --> 00:04:00.138
In a country not generally known

00:04:00.418 --> 00:04:02.818
for its bureaucratic efficiency, it

00:04:02.818 --> 00:04:05.108
seems slightly suspicious that the

00:04:05.108 --> 00:04:07.358
death is announced at almost the

00:04:07.358 --> 00:04:10.128
exact moment of the body's discovery.

00:04:11.138 --> 00:04:13.818
And this is far from the only

00:04:13.848 --> 00:04:15.818
unusual aspect to this story.

00:04:16.808 --> 00:04:19.958
Now, to understand why so few people

00:04:20.018 --> 00:04:23.018
accept the official story of how he died,

00:04:23.308 --> 00:04:26.338
we first have to understand who he was.

00:04:27.348 --> 00:04:30.470
Pasolini was born in March, 1922,

00:04:30.788 --> 00:04:33.168
in Bologna, in the north of Italy.

00:04:34.028 --> 00:04:36.428
This, as the history buffs among

00:04:36.428 --> 00:04:39.468
you may know, was just a few months

00:04:39.598 --> 00:04:41.978
before Mussolini's March on Rome,

00:04:42.218 --> 00:04:44.558
and Italy falling to fascism.

00:04:45.458 --> 00:04:48.758
And fascism, both his experience living

00:04:48.868 --> 00:04:52.058
under it in his adolescence, and under

00:04:52.058 --> 00:04:54.518
the relics of fascism after it fell,

00:04:54.958 --> 00:04:57.278
well, it would permeate everything:

00:04:57.608 --> 00:05:00.088
his personal life, his political

00:05:00.088 --> 00:05:03.088
views, his philosophy, and his work.

00:05:04.208 --> 00:05:06.908
Pasolini was a creative, an

00:05:06.928 --> 00:05:09.458
idealist, someone who started

00:05:09.458 --> 00:05:12.078
writing poetry at the age of seven.

00:05:12.808 --> 00:05:16.278
He took great joy in natural beauty,

00:05:16.438 --> 00:05:19.878
in the variety of human experience.

00:05:21.138 --> 00:05:23.208
This, as you might imagine,

00:05:23.618 --> 00:05:25.488
put him at odds with the

00:05:25.488 --> 00:05:28.048
entire philosophy of fascism.

00:05:29.328 --> 00:05:31.918
One of the defining principles of

00:05:31.948 --> 00:05:34.888
Italian fascism was the unification

00:05:35.208 --> 00:05:37.798
and standardisation of everything.

00:05:39.288 --> 00:05:41.228
Mussolini believed this was

00:05:41.258 --> 00:05:43.638
particularly important because it

00:05:43.638 --> 00:05:46.718
would create a sense of national unity.

00:05:47.898 --> 00:05:51.178
Italy, remember, had only become a unified

00:05:51.228 --> 00:05:54.958
country in 1861, sixty years earlier.

00:05:56.318 --> 00:05:58.898
Before then, the Italian peninsula

00:05:59.198 --> 00:06:01.358
had been a patchwork of kingdoms

00:06:01.358 --> 00:06:03.338
and territories, each with its

00:06:03.338 --> 00:06:05.758
own laws, its own traditions, its

00:06:05.758 --> 00:06:08.568
own culture, and its own language.

00:06:09.578 --> 00:06:12.598
For many Italians, standard Italian

00:06:12.948 --> 00:06:16.008
was still more a written, official

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:18.298
language than the language of daily life.

00:06:19.238 --> 00:06:22.138
Most people spoke their regional dialect.

00:06:22.688 --> 00:06:24.608
If you put a farmer from the

00:06:24.618 --> 00:06:26.498
Dolomites, in the north east of the

00:06:26.498 --> 00:06:28.708
country, together with a shepherd

00:06:28.708 --> 00:06:30.798
from Sicily, they would have barely

00:06:30.798 --> 00:06:32.518
been able to understand one another.

00:06:34.088 --> 00:06:36.008
The fascist regime tried

00:06:36.038 --> 00:06:38.078
its utmost to change this.

00:06:39.388 --> 00:06:41.298
Mussolini launched what he

00:06:41.298 --> 00:06:43.408
called the bonifica linguistica.

00:06:44.728 --> 00:06:46.928
Foreign words were expelled

00:06:46.988 --> 00:06:48.538
from public spaces.

00:06:48.808 --> 00:06:51.958
Dialects were stigmatised in schools.

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:54.208
The state radio was in a

00:06:54.208 --> 00:06:56.898
standardised, centralised Italian.

00:06:58.508 --> 00:07:02.048
To a young Pasolini, this was an assault

00:07:02.248 --> 00:07:05.408
on everything he believed in: uniqueness,

00:07:05.788 --> 00:07:09.598
individuality, the beauty of variety.

00:07:10.838 --> 00:07:12.998
And this variety was something

00:07:12.998 --> 00:07:14.768
he was acutely aware of.

00:07:15.918 --> 00:07:18.928
His mother came from Friuli, a

00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:21.318
largely agricultural region in the

00:07:21.378 --> 00:07:23.598
far north-east of Italy, near the

00:07:23.598 --> 00:07:25.968
border with what was then Yugoslavia.

00:07:27.188 --> 00:07:28.758
He had lived there as a child,

00:07:29.098 --> 00:07:30.838
visiting the small farming

00:07:30.838 --> 00:07:32.568
villages where she had grown up.

00:07:33.248 --> 00:07:36.328
And in those villages, he had

00:07:36.328 --> 00:07:39.248
heard Friulano, the local dialect.

00:07:40.588 --> 00:07:42.768
He couldn't speak Friulano himself,

00:07:43.018 --> 00:07:45.108
but there was something about

00:07:45.118 --> 00:07:47.848
it that seemed to draw him in.

00:07:49.078 --> 00:07:52.048
He ended up writing poetry in Friulano,

00:07:52.478 --> 00:07:55.178
and his first poetry collection was

00:07:55.178 --> 00:07:59.048
published entirely in Friulano, this quite

00:07:59.098 --> 00:08:02.308
obscure dialect spoken mainly by peasants.

00:08:03.448 --> 00:08:06.378
And apart from the linguistic

00:08:06.388 --> 00:08:08.708
vandalism he felt the fascists were

00:08:08.708 --> 00:08:11.958
pursuing, fascism affected him in

00:08:12.058 --> 00:08:14.978
other, dare I say, more serious ways.

00:08:16.528 --> 00:08:18.358
His younger brother, Guido,

00:08:18.678 --> 00:08:20.658
had joined the partisans, the

00:08:20.878 --> 00:08:23.238
resistance fighters against fascism.

00:08:24.178 --> 00:08:27.378
And in February 1945, with the

00:08:27.378 --> 00:08:30.558
war almost over, Guido was killed.

00:08:31.488 --> 00:08:33.048
He was not killed by the

00:08:33.058 --> 00:08:34.878
Germans, or by the fascists.

00:08:35.158 --> 00:08:37.898
He was killed in a massacre carried

00:08:37.898 --> 00:08:40.808
out by a rival partisan faction.

00:08:42.328 --> 00:08:45.388
Pasolini blamed the fascists for dragging

00:08:45.398 --> 00:08:47.688
Italy into the war in the first place.

00:08:48.008 --> 00:08:51.588
But he also blamed the left, whose ugly

00:08:51.628 --> 00:08:54.028
infighting had left his brother dead.

00:08:54.838 --> 00:08:57.368
This is important, as it foreshadows

00:08:57.698 --> 00:08:59.628
Pasolini not really feeling at home

00:08:59.898 --> 00:09:02.168
in any traditional political category.

00:09:03.468 --> 00:09:06.428
Still, after the war, Pasolini

00:09:06.458 --> 00:09:08.568
joined the Italian Communist Party,

00:09:08.918 --> 00:09:11.368
as the clear opposite to fascism.

00:09:12.238 --> 00:09:14.608
He was an active local member: he

00:09:14.608 --> 00:09:17.058
organised peasants, attended rallies,

00:09:17.278 --> 00:09:19.578
and wrote for party publications.

00:09:20.208 --> 00:09:22.908
He was, for the first and last time

00:09:22.908 --> 00:09:25.598
in his life, a semi-conventional

00:09:25.628 --> 00:09:27.928
political creature, a card-carrying

00:09:27.928 --> 00:09:30.368
member of the Italian Communist Party.

00:09:31.118 --> 00:09:33.168
But it did not last.

00:09:34.038 --> 00:09:38.138
In September 1949, at a local festival

00:09:38.148 --> 00:09:40.708
in a village, he was accused of

00:09:40.718 --> 00:09:42.878
having been involved in a sexual

00:09:42.898 --> 00:09:45.928
encounter with several teenage boys.

00:09:46.798 --> 00:09:48.768
He was charged with corruption

00:09:48.788 --> 00:09:51.578
of minors and public obscenity.

00:09:52.828 --> 00:09:54.230
He was later acquitted.

00:09:54.504 --> 00:09:58.118
But the damage was done, and the scandal,

00:09:58.448 --> 00:10:00.538
and the stigma around his sexuality,

00:10:00.838 --> 00:10:03.588
would follow him for the rest of his life.

00:10:04.338 --> 00:10:06.748
He was fired from his job and kicked

00:10:06.778 --> 00:10:09.038
out of the Italian Communist Party.

00:10:09.648 --> 00:10:11.588
The official statement spoke

00:10:11.608 --> 00:10:13.858
of "deleterious influences"

00:10:14.118 --> 00:10:16.408
and "bourgeois degeneration."

00:10:17.278 --> 00:10:20.168
He packed his things, and in January

00:10:20.178 --> 00:10:23.738
1950, he moved to Rome with his mother.

00:10:24.678 --> 00:10:28.778
Now, at first, Rome was not kind to them.

00:10:29.408 --> 00:10:31.618
They arrived with very little money.

00:10:32.308 --> 00:10:34.378
Pasolini worked whatever jobs

00:10:34.378 --> 00:10:36.638
he could find: private tutoring,

00:10:37.017 --> 00:10:38.858
writing small commissions, and

00:10:39.118 --> 00:10:41.458
bit parts as an extra in films.

00:10:42.728 --> 00:10:44.908
He and his mother lived in cheap

00:10:44.928 --> 00:10:47.208
accommodation on the outskirts of the

00:10:47.208 --> 00:10:49.338
city, in the kind of neighbourhoods

00:10:49.588 --> 00:10:51.418
that you won't find on a postcard.

00:10:52.688 --> 00:10:55.468
These were called the borgate, and

00:10:55.708 --> 00:10:57.878
they were the poor suburbs of Rome,

00:10:58.268 --> 00:11:01.338
often grey, dusty places without

00:11:01.338 --> 00:11:03.298
proper sanitation or services.

00:11:04.018 --> 00:11:05.908
These were not places that

00:11:06.078 --> 00:11:08.228
young, university-educated

00:11:08.248 --> 00:11:10.518
intellectuals tended to frequent,

00:11:11.028 --> 00:11:13.858
but Pasolini felt differently.

00:11:14.788 --> 00:11:18.268
While others saw misery and simplicity,

00:11:18.768 --> 00:11:21.528
he found a kind of vitality.

00:11:21.838 --> 00:11:24.588
A rawness, a directness, a

00:11:24.598 --> 00:11:26.928
resistance to the flattening

00:11:26.928 --> 00:11:29.378
forces of modern Italian life.

00:11:30.288 --> 00:11:32.228
The young men who idled on the

00:11:32.228 --> 00:11:35.438
streets spoke in thick Roman dialect.

00:11:35.758 --> 00:11:37.418
They had their own codes, their

00:11:37.698 --> 00:11:40.098
own dark humour, their own beauty.

00:11:41.358 --> 00:11:43.118
He wrote two novels about them.

00:11:43.728 --> 00:11:45.568
Both scandalised the

00:11:45.598 --> 00:11:46.767
literary establishment.

00:11:47.098 --> 00:11:49.228
And both sold very well.

00:11:50.248 --> 00:11:52.678
And then he turned to film.

00:11:53.488 --> 00:11:56.748
Now, this episode would be hours long if

00:11:56.748 --> 00:12:00.227
we went into every single one, but suffice

00:12:00.258 --> 00:12:02.878
it to say, his films were controversial.

00:12:03.368 --> 00:12:06.168
They ruffled more than a few feathers, and

00:12:06.298 --> 00:12:09.048
he was prosecuted for nearly all of them.

00:12:09.728 --> 00:12:10.488
Obscenity.

00:12:10.748 --> 00:12:11.488
Blasphemy.

00:12:11.758 --> 00:12:13.128
Contempt for religion.

00:12:13.968 --> 00:12:16.568
He was never convicted, but each

00:12:16.568 --> 00:12:18.648
one brought him new enemies.

00:12:19.428 --> 00:12:22.218
Many in the Catholic Church despised

00:12:22.218 --> 00:12:25.278
his work, as did many on the right.

00:12:26.388 --> 00:12:29.598
And in 1968, he did something

00:12:29.858 --> 00:12:31.328
that cost him most of his

00:12:31.328 --> 00:12:33.338
remaining allies on the left.

00:12:34.758 --> 00:12:38.478
That spring, students across Italy — and

00:12:38.488 --> 00:12:41.218
across much of Europe — had been rioting.

00:12:42.508 --> 00:12:44.988
In Rome, there had been a violent

00:12:45.078 --> 00:12:47.668
clash between students and police

00:12:47.988 --> 00:12:49.818
at a place called Valle Giulia.

00:12:51.408 --> 00:12:53.808
You might have expected Pasolini to

00:12:53.808 --> 00:12:55.498
have sympathised with the students.

00:12:56.098 --> 00:12:58.118
After all, they were traditionally

00:12:58.128 --> 00:12:59.908
associated with the left, and

00:12:59.908 --> 00:13:01.298
the police were traditionally

00:13:01.298 --> 00:13:03.008
associated with the right.

00:13:03.668 --> 00:13:07.378
But Pasolini came out in sympathy not

00:13:07.408 --> 00:13:09.908
with the students, but with the police.

00:13:11.408 --> 00:13:14.388
He described the students as spoiled

00:13:14.618 --> 00:13:17.058
and bourgeois, children of wealthy

00:13:17.058 --> 00:13:19.758
families playing at revolution.

00:13:20.748 --> 00:13:22.638
The police, on the other hand,

00:13:23.008 --> 00:13:25.018
were young men who had grown up

00:13:25.028 --> 00:13:27.488
in poverty and joined the police

00:13:27.648 --> 00:13:29.628
because there was no alternative.

00:13:30.438 --> 00:13:32.538
For all their talk of revolution,

00:13:32.918 --> 00:13:35.258
the students had the same instinct

00:13:35.288 --> 00:13:37.258
as the fascists they claimed to

00:13:37.258 --> 00:13:39.818
oppose: they wanted everyone to

00:13:39.828 --> 00:13:42.028
think the same way, and they were

00:13:42.028 --> 00:13:44.688
ready to turn on anyone who didn't.

00:13:45.538 --> 00:13:48.178
He called it left-wing fascism.

00:13:49.518 --> 00:13:52.698
And moving into the 1970s, he

00:13:52.698 --> 00:13:55.298
became increasingly vocal about

00:13:55.298 --> 00:13:57.728
what he saw as the cultural

00:13:57.938 --> 00:14:00.638
catastrophe of his beloved country.

00:14:01.838 --> 00:14:04.398
In the two decades after the war,

00:14:04.938 --> 00:14:06.958
the Italy Pasolini had grown up in

00:14:07.328 --> 00:14:10.128
had changed with remarkable speed.

00:14:11.068 --> 00:14:13.358
The economic boom of the 1950s and

00:14:13.358 --> 00:14:16.758
1960s had brought industry, television,

00:14:17.158 --> 00:14:19.768
rising wages, and mass consumption.

00:14:20.848 --> 00:14:22.868
By almost any conventional

00:14:22.868 --> 00:14:25.978
measure, most Italians were better

00:14:25.978 --> 00:14:27.878
off than they had ever been.

00:14:28.908 --> 00:14:31.668
Yet to Pasolini, things

00:14:31.888 --> 00:14:33.398
had never been worse.

00:14:34.598 --> 00:14:38.388
For all its brutality, he said, fascism

00:14:38.678 --> 00:14:42.108
had never truly remade ordinary Italians.

00:14:42.618 --> 00:14:43.878
It had demanded their

00:14:43.888 --> 00:14:46.098
obedience, but not their souls.

00:14:47.108 --> 00:14:49.448
Consumer capitalism, television,

00:14:49.758 --> 00:14:52.278
advertising, the ideology of

00:14:52.278 --> 00:14:55.198
consumption: these had achieved

00:14:55.268 --> 00:14:57.378
what Mussolini never managed.

00:14:57.748 --> 00:14:59.248
They had flattened Italy.

00:14:59.698 --> 00:15:02.268
They had eroded the regional cultures,

00:15:02.498 --> 00:15:04.568
the local dialects, the particular

00:15:04.568 --> 00:15:07.078
identities that made each part of the

00:15:07.078 --> 00:15:09.418
country different from every other.

00:15:10.648 --> 00:15:12.758
In their place had come a

00:15:12.808 --> 00:15:15.928
single, homogenised, nationally

00:15:15.928 --> 00:15:17.528
standardised way of being.

00:15:19.118 --> 00:15:22.528
He called this the new fascism: it

00:15:22.528 --> 00:15:25.088
wasn't the fascism of uniforms and

00:15:25.088 --> 00:15:27.428
rallies, the fascism of Mussolini.

00:15:27.928 --> 00:15:30.728
This was a fascism that worked through

00:15:30.818 --> 00:15:34.068
pleasure and desire rather than coercion.

00:15:35.398 --> 00:15:37.498
And it was not only consumer

00:15:37.538 --> 00:15:39.768
culture that Pasolini was attacking.

00:15:40.908 --> 00:15:44.778
In November 1974, he published an article

00:15:45.038 --> 00:15:47.438
that became immediately notorious.

00:15:48.028 --> 00:15:49.881
It was called "Io so".

00:15:50.848 --> 00:15:51.508
"I know."

00:15:52.608 --> 00:15:56.188
In it, he wrote that he knew who

00:15:56.198 --> 00:15:58.508
was responsible for the political

00:15:58.518 --> 00:16:01.048
violence of the previous decade: the

00:16:01.048 --> 00:16:03.368
bombings, the assassinations, the

00:16:03.478 --> 00:16:05.598
"strategy of tension" that had killed

00:16:05.608 --> 00:16:08.408
dozens of people, the "Years of Lead",

00:16:08.508 --> 00:16:10.058
which you might remember from our

00:16:10.058 --> 00:16:11.798
mini-series at the end of last year.

00:16:13.048 --> 00:16:14.718
"I know," he wrote,

00:16:15.258 --> 00:16:17.138
"but I have no evidence.

00:16:17.498 --> 00:16:19.138
Not even clues."

00:16:20.298 --> 00:16:22.678
In this article, he claimed to

00:16:22.678 --> 00:16:25.148
know exactly who was responsible,

00:16:25.338 --> 00:16:27.098
who was pulling the strings,

00:16:27.298 --> 00:16:29.708
yet admitted he had no proof.

00:16:30.228 --> 00:16:32.758
Whatever he actually knew, the

00:16:32.758 --> 00:16:35.058
article made him powerful enemies.

00:16:35.818 --> 00:16:38.808
And by this point, Pasolini was

00:16:38.818 --> 00:16:41.408
almost impossible to categorise:

00:16:41.828 --> 00:16:43.948
too communist for conservatives,

00:16:44.148 --> 00:16:46.488
too conservative for communists, too

00:16:46.498 --> 00:16:48.968
religious for secular intellectuals,

00:16:49.298 --> 00:16:51.998
and too scandalous for the religious.

00:16:52.398 --> 00:16:55.368
An openly homosexual man in a still

00:16:55.538 --> 00:16:57.398
religiously conservative country.

00:16:58.828 --> 00:17:01.508
Now, we must move forward towards

00:17:01.508 --> 00:17:03.748
the evening of the 1st of November,

00:17:04.218 --> 00:17:06.428
All Saints' Day, and the last

00:17:06.478 --> 00:17:08.478
evening of Pasolini's life.

00:17:09.798 --> 00:17:12.288
He had recently finished a film called

00:17:12.518 --> 00:17:15.918
"Salò, or the 120 Days of Sodom".

00:17:16.708 --> 00:17:19.588
It is the most extreme and deliberately

00:17:19.822 --> 00:17:22.218
unwatchable film he ever made.

00:17:22.988 --> 00:17:26.498
It was loosely based on a work by

00:17:26.498 --> 00:17:29.068
the Marquis de Sade, and set in

00:17:29.068 --> 00:17:31.178
the final months of Mussolini's

00:17:31.208 --> 00:17:33.828
Republic of Salò in 1944.

00:17:35.148 --> 00:17:38.498
In it, four powerful men abduct

00:17:38.608 --> 00:17:41.378
eighteen teenagers, take them to an

00:17:41.448 --> 00:17:44.458
isolated villa, and then subject them

00:17:44.458 --> 00:17:47.858
to systematic torture, rape, and murder.

00:17:48.908 --> 00:17:51.388
It is a film about absolute power,

00:17:51.818 --> 00:17:54.048
about what a ruling class does to

00:17:54.048 --> 00:17:56.028
those it controls when it believes

00:17:56.058 --> 00:17:58.238
it will never be called to account.

00:17:59.218 --> 00:18:02.238
Earlier that year, in August, something

00:18:02.388 --> 00:18:04.648
had happened to those film reels.

00:18:05.188 --> 00:18:05.878
A theft.

00:18:06.658 --> 00:18:09.998
Reels from Salò were stolen, and

00:18:10.238 --> 00:18:12.798
this being 1975, there were no

00:18:12.798 --> 00:18:15.388
hard drives or reusable backups.

00:18:16.448 --> 00:18:18.668
The thieves demanded a ransom.

00:18:19.708 --> 00:18:23.318
Pasolini refused to pay and instead

00:18:23.748 --> 00:18:25.998
had the affected scenes reshot.

00:18:26.848 --> 00:18:29.038
But he still wanted them back.

00:18:29.898 --> 00:18:32.708
Among the missing footage was a scene in

00:18:32.708 --> 00:18:35.508
which Pasolini himself appeared, dancing.

00:18:36.138 --> 00:18:38.378
He was desperate to get it back.

00:18:39.348 --> 00:18:42.368
Now, the reason to mention this is because

00:18:42.378 --> 00:18:45.558
one theory has it that the reason Pasolini

00:18:45.588 --> 00:18:48.388
went to Ostia on the night of his murder

00:18:48.808 --> 00:18:51.178
was to get these stolen reels back,

00:18:51.538 --> 00:18:55.398
and he somehow walked into an ambush.

00:18:57.078 --> 00:19:00.868
To this day, it is unclear exactly

00:19:00.998 --> 00:19:03.088
what happened to him on the night of

00:19:03.088 --> 00:19:06.538
1st November, who killed him, and why.

00:19:08.498 --> 00:19:11.328
Pelosi, the 17-year-old who was

00:19:11.328 --> 00:19:13.938
found speeding in his car, told

00:19:13.948 --> 00:19:16.068
his version of events when he was

00:19:16.068 --> 00:19:18.588
formally questioned after his arrest.

00:19:19.948 --> 00:19:22.748
He said Pasolini had approached him

00:19:22.778 --> 00:19:24.898
and a group of friends outside a

00:19:24.908 --> 00:19:27.918
bar near Termini station, and asked

00:19:27.928 --> 00:19:30.308
if anyone wanted to "fare un giro".

00:19:31.798 --> 00:19:34.178
Now, this expression is actually

00:19:34.208 --> 00:19:36.488
quite a hard thing to directly

00:19:36.488 --> 00:19:38.698
translate into English, but is

00:19:38.817 --> 00:19:41.237
something like "to go for a ride".

00:19:42.398 --> 00:19:45.548
And coming from Pasolini to a group of

00:19:45.548 --> 00:19:47.788
young men hanging out near the train

00:19:47.788 --> 00:19:50.378
station late at night, there would have

00:19:50.378 --> 00:19:53.148
been an implied sexual connotation.

00:19:54.208 --> 00:19:57.098
Pelosi, who was a street kid and

00:19:57.098 --> 00:19:59.188
prepared to do whatever was necessary

00:19:59.408 --> 00:20:02.148
to make some money, well, he took him

00:20:02.148 --> 00:20:04.913
up on the offer and got into the car.

00:20:05.797 --> 00:20:08.218
Pelosi told the older man that

00:20:08.218 --> 00:20:11.208
he was starving, so the pair went

00:20:11.208 --> 00:20:12.808
for a late-night meal, with the

00:20:12.808 --> 00:20:14.848
young man having a bowl of pasta

00:20:15.078 --> 00:20:16.888
and then a slice of chicken, and

00:20:17.218 --> 00:20:18.838
Pasolini just drinking a beer.

00:20:20.078 --> 00:20:23.638
Then, Pasolini drove him out to Ostia, on

00:20:23.638 --> 00:20:26.448
the coast, around 30 kilometres from Rome.

00:20:27.408 --> 00:20:29.738
When they arrived, Pasolini

00:20:29.878 --> 00:20:32.328
requested sexual acts from him

00:20:32.668 --> 00:20:33.888
that he did not want to do.

00:20:34.698 --> 00:20:35.678
There was an argument.

00:20:36.278 --> 00:20:37.808
Pasolini struck him first

00:20:37.808 --> 00:20:39.188
with a stick, he said.

00:20:39.905 --> 00:20:41.978
Pelosi then grabbed a wooden

00:20:41.978 --> 00:20:43.618
board and fought back.

00:20:44.768 --> 00:20:46.628
Somehow, the pair were

00:20:46.818 --> 00:20:48.498
outside the car at this point.

00:20:49.115 --> 00:20:51.398
Pelosi then ran back to the car

00:20:51.598 --> 00:20:53.848
and drove away, trying to escape.

00:20:54.488 --> 00:20:56.878
He says he ran over Pasolini's

00:20:57.038 --> 00:20:59.788
body by accident as he drove off.

00:21:00.358 --> 00:21:02.978
A sexual encounter gone horribly wrong.

00:21:03.438 --> 00:21:04.108
Nothing more.

00:21:05.658 --> 00:21:08.018
Now, there are more than a

00:21:08.028 --> 00:21:09.818
few problems with this story.

00:21:10.978 --> 00:21:13.428
The first is Pasolini himself.

00:21:13.918 --> 00:21:16.468
He was lean, athletic,

00:21:16.708 --> 00:21:18.508
and experienced in karate.

00:21:19.228 --> 00:21:21.378
Pino Pelosi was a skinny

00:21:21.548 --> 00:21:22.608
seventeen-year-old.

00:21:23.708 --> 00:21:25.428
In a straight fight between the

00:21:25.428 --> 00:21:27.908
two of them, it is hard to see how

00:21:27.908 --> 00:21:29.998
Pelosi would have come out on top.

00:21:31.198 --> 00:21:34.378
The second problem is Pasolini's injuries.

00:21:34.988 --> 00:21:37.358
He had multiple broken bones,

00:21:37.438 --> 00:21:39.908
crushed ribs, and his body had

00:21:39.908 --> 00:21:42.308
been run over repeatedly by a car.

00:21:43.318 --> 00:21:45.868
The injuries simply didn't tally

00:21:46.048 --> 00:21:48.338
with what Pelosi said happened, that

00:21:48.458 --> 00:21:50.228
he had been hit by a wooden board.

00:21:51.418 --> 00:21:53.998
The third problem is Pelosi himself.

00:21:54.858 --> 00:21:56.498
When the police stopped him that

00:21:56.498 --> 00:21:59.348
night, he was almost unmarked.

00:21:59.898 --> 00:22:01.848
There were none of the cuts or

00:22:01.848 --> 00:22:03.678
bruises you would expect on someone

00:22:03.948 --> 00:22:05.708
who had just fought a grown man to

00:22:05.708 --> 00:22:08.588
the death, and not a single trace

00:22:08.768 --> 00:22:11.108
of Pasolini's blood on his clothes.

00:22:12.228 --> 00:22:14.388
The fourth problem is the timing.

00:22:15.428 --> 00:22:17.925
When Pelosi was arrested at 1:30

00:22:17.935 --> 00:22:20.138
in the morning, the police went to

00:22:20.138 --> 00:22:22.218
Pasolini's apartment and told his

00:22:22.218 --> 00:22:24.088
family the car had been stolen.

00:22:24.648 --> 00:22:26.858
At least, that was the official version.

00:22:28.037 --> 00:22:31.328
At 6:30 AM, the body was found, and

00:22:31.548 --> 00:22:34.348
at that exact moment, Italian state

00:22:34.348 --> 00:22:37.078
radio broadcast the news of the murder.

00:22:37.908 --> 00:22:40.778
This suggests that someone had that

00:22:40.798 --> 00:22:43.468
information before the official

00:22:43.468 --> 00:22:45.958
discovery of the body, and had

00:22:45.958 --> 00:22:47.748
held it until the moment it could

00:22:47.748 --> 00:22:49.528
appear to have come from the scene.

00:22:50.648 --> 00:22:53.398
Who knew what, and when,

00:22:53.918 --> 00:22:55.058
has never been answered.

00:22:56.198 --> 00:22:57.898
There are other questions too.

00:22:58.608 --> 00:23:01.848
Why was Pelosi so desperate to inform the

00:23:01.848 --> 00:23:04.268
police about the ring, a ring that would

00:23:04.278 --> 00:23:06.328
directly implicate him in the murder?

00:23:07.188 --> 00:23:10.098
And friends say that this ring never

00:23:10.268 --> 00:23:13.328
left Pelosi's finger, so it seemed

00:23:13.338 --> 00:23:15.828
a little suspicious, convenient,

00:23:15.858 --> 00:23:18.038
even, for it to be found right

00:23:18.038 --> 00:23:20.698
next to Pasolini's lifeless corpse.

00:23:21.098 --> 00:23:22.928
As if the young man was

00:23:22.938 --> 00:23:24.838
being deliberately set up.

00:23:26.038 --> 00:23:28.008
And there is more to suggest

00:23:28.008 --> 00:23:29.858
that Pelosi was not alone that

00:23:29.858 --> 00:23:31.948
night, or even there at all.

00:23:33.008 --> 00:23:34.968
The owners of the restaurant where

00:23:35.028 --> 00:23:37.688
Pasolini had his last meal said

00:23:37.698 --> 00:23:39.768
the young man at his table did

00:23:39.768 --> 00:23:41.498
not match Pelosi's description.

00:23:42.818 --> 00:23:45.428
And on the morning the body was found,

00:23:45.858 --> 00:23:48.128
a local man told a journalist on the

00:23:48.128 --> 00:23:51.308
scene that he had seen four or five

00:23:51.318 --> 00:23:53.658
people around Pasolini that night.

00:23:54.088 --> 00:23:55.968
They massacred that poor man

00:23:56.208 --> 00:23:57.778
for half an hour, he said.

00:23:58.188 --> 00:23:59.968
He cried mamma, mamma, mamma.

00:24:01.288 --> 00:24:04.138
It seemed to be a pretty damning witness

00:24:04.138 --> 00:24:06.838
statement, but after the statement

00:24:07.018 --> 00:24:09.048
and the man's identity were made

00:24:09.078 --> 00:24:12.138
public, he refused to say another word.

00:24:13.398 --> 00:24:16.576
Pelosi was convicted in 1976,

00:24:16.818 --> 00:24:19.288
but the judge wasn't convinced by

00:24:19.288 --> 00:24:22.028
his story either, writing, "the

00:24:22.028 --> 00:24:24.578
record overwhelmingly shows that on

00:24:24.578 --> 00:24:27.108
that night Pelosi was not alone."

00:24:28.508 --> 00:24:30.948
Now, Pelosi stuck to this

00:24:30.948 --> 00:24:32.968
story, for a while at least.

00:24:33.718 --> 00:24:38.068
But in 2005, 30 years later,

00:24:38.438 --> 00:24:40.828
he retracted his confession.

00:24:41.678 --> 00:24:43.798
He said that he had driven to

00:24:43.808 --> 00:24:46.598
Ostia with Pasolini, but they had

00:24:46.628 --> 00:24:48.978
been ambushed by a group of men,

00:24:49.458 --> 00:24:51.698
three, perhaps even five of them.

00:24:52.608 --> 00:24:56.248
Men he described as neo-fascists, with

00:24:56.258 --> 00:24:58.698
southern Italian accents, who had beaten

00:24:58.748 --> 00:25:00.928
Pasolini while screaming insults at

00:25:00.928 --> 00:25:03.648
him, calling him a dirty communist.

00:25:04.598 --> 00:25:07.588
Pelosi had taken the blame, he claimed,

00:25:07.868 --> 00:25:09.968
because they had threatened his family.

00:25:11.448 --> 00:25:16.268
And indeed, in 2014, DNA analysis

00:25:16.418 --> 00:25:18.438
of the clothes Pasolini was wearing

00:25:18.438 --> 00:25:21.138
that night found traces from at

00:25:21.148 --> 00:25:23.588
least three separate individuals.

00:25:25.288 --> 00:25:28.518
As for who those individuals were and who

00:25:28.518 --> 00:25:31.418
sent them, that is where it's easy to go

00:25:31.418 --> 00:25:33.738
very deep down different rabbit holes.

00:25:35.128 --> 00:25:37.368
Investigators have pointed towards

00:25:37.368 --> 00:25:39.778
the Banda della Magliana, a powerful

00:25:39.778 --> 00:25:42.158
criminal organisation with documented

00:25:42.158 --> 00:25:45.488
ties to far-right extremism and to

00:25:45.518 --> 00:25:47.958
a secret network called Propaganda

00:25:47.968 --> 00:25:50.778
Due — P2 [P-Two] for short.

00:25:52.258 --> 00:25:54.068
In other words, he was killed

00:25:54.328 --> 00:25:56.488
either by right-wing extremists

00:25:56.708 --> 00:25:59.008
or by mysterious puppet masters

00:25:59.198 --> 00:26:02.218
linked to the P2 Masonic lodge.

00:26:03.058 --> 00:26:05.479
There's also the theory that he was

00:26:05.489 --> 00:26:07.979
murdered because of what he was about

00:26:07.979 --> 00:26:11.329
to publish in an unfinished novel called

00:26:11.399 --> 00:26:14.659
Petrolio, or "Oil", which spoke of

00:26:14.669 --> 00:26:16.839
government and corporate corruption.

00:26:18.219 --> 00:26:20.369
And indeed, when it was recovered

00:26:20.439 --> 00:26:22.748
after his death, the manuscript

00:26:22.918 --> 00:26:25.498
had a missing section, a section

00:26:25.698 --> 00:26:28.528
which some have speculated contained

00:26:28.618 --> 00:26:30.388
politically sensitive material.

00:26:31.458 --> 00:26:34.388
Or was it simply a robbery gone wrong?

00:26:34.888 --> 00:26:36.838
A group of criminals trying to

00:26:36.888 --> 00:26:38.848
dispossess a man of his belongings,

00:26:39.138 --> 00:26:41.138
a strong man who had fought back,

00:26:41.448 --> 00:26:43.098
and it had ended in tragedy?

00:26:44.748 --> 00:26:48.311
For what it's worth, in December 2022,

00:26:48.788 --> 00:26:51.478
the Italian Anti-Mafia Parliamentary

00:26:51.478 --> 00:26:54.158
Commission concluded that major

00:26:54.178 --> 00:26:57.228
criminal gangs, including the Banda

00:26:57.228 --> 00:27:00.308
della Magliana, may have been involved

00:27:00.318 --> 00:27:03.498
in the murder, and noted serious

00:27:03.508 --> 00:27:05.858
failures in the original investigation.

00:27:06.988 --> 00:27:09.418
The commission said it was unlikely the

00:27:09.468 --> 00:27:12.168
perpetrators would ever face justice.

00:27:13.238 --> 00:27:16.864
And when Pino Pelosi died in 2017,

00:27:17.378 --> 00:27:19.038
the chances of the truth coming

00:27:19.038 --> 00:27:21.078
to light became even slimmer.

00:27:22.578 --> 00:27:24.708
As of today, the case

00:27:24.768 --> 00:27:26.638
remains officially unsolved.

00:27:27.468 --> 00:27:30.218
Fifty years on, the man who never

00:27:30.218 --> 00:27:32.648
fit a single category has a death

00:27:32.888 --> 00:27:34.118
that doesn't fit one either.

00:27:34.948 --> 00:27:37.558
Italy has been arguing about it ever

00:27:37.558 --> 00:27:40.528
since, and is no closer to an answer.

00:27:42.398 --> 00:27:44.558
OK, then, that is it for today's

00:27:44.558 --> 00:27:46.528
episode on Pier Paolo Pasolini.

00:27:46.848 --> 00:27:48.318
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:27:48.378 --> 00:27:50.028
and that you've learnt something new.

00:27:50.608 --> 00:27:52.968
This period of Italian history is a

00:27:52.998 --> 00:27:55.468
veritable treasure trove of these sorts

00:27:55.488 --> 00:27:57.448
of mysteries, and if you enjoyed this

00:27:57.448 --> 00:27:59.778
one, I'd recommend our mini-series on

00:27:59.778 --> 00:28:03.058
the Years of Lead, which is episodes 582

00:28:03.058 --> 00:28:07.948
to 584, episode 147, about the murder

00:28:07.968 --> 00:28:12.098
of Roberto Calvi, and episode 63, about

00:28:12.148 --> 00:28:14.549
the disappearance of Emanuela Orlandi.

00:28:15.156 --> 00:28:17.716
And if you are an Italian speaker, there

00:28:17.716 --> 00:28:20.876
is an entire drama podcast series called

00:28:21.076 --> 00:28:23.436
Feroce - L'ultima Notte di Pasolini, which

00:28:23.446 --> 00:28:26.206
is very good, and goes into the story in

00:28:26.206 --> 00:28:28.296
ways we didn't have the time to do today.

00:28:29.216 --> 00:28:31.086
You've been listening to English Learning

00:28:31.086 --> 00:28:33.776
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:28:34.076 --> 00:28:36.946
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:28:36.966 --> 00:28:39.276
I'll catch you in the next episode.

